1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,512
Welcome to Module Twenty-Seven.

2
00:00:02,512 --> 00:00:05,850
A breeder never designs a new animal from scratch.

3
00:00:05,850 --> 00:00:12,525
They start from a stock that already has most of what they want, and they shift it, carefully, towards one trait.

4
00:00:12,525 --> 00:00:17,362
And much of what a young animal knows, it never got from selection at all.

5
00:00:17,362 --> 00:00:19,825
It learned by imitating its parents.

6
00:00:19,825 --> 00:00:21,275
Song dialects.

7
00:00:21,275 --> 00:00:22,675
Foraging tricks.

8
00:00:22,675 --> 00:00:24,225
Migration routes.

9
00:00:24,225 --> 00:00:31,300
Cultural transmission is fast and faithful, and it passes on the parents' mistakes right along with their skills.

10
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:35,125
Supervised fine-tuning is cultural transmission for a model.

11
00:00:35,125 --> 00:00:40,150
You show it examples of the behaviour you want, and you nudge its weights so it imitates them.

12
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:44,850
A lecture in this pack describes it in one phrase: imitation learning.

13
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:51,937
The model is graded on every word of the example answers, and over many examples it learns to produce answers like them.

14
00:00:51,937 --> 00:00:54,362
The lecture also lists the catches.

15
00:00:54,362 --> 00:00:56,762
A student can't outgrow its teacher.

16
00:00:56,762 --> 00:01:00,312
Wrong examples are learned as faithfully as right ones.

17
00:01:00,312 --> 00:01:03,700
Even a successful example can carry bad habits.

18
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:09,475
Adding more of the same examples stops helping after a while, while new kinds of examples keep helping.

19
00:01:09,475 --> 00:01:13,612
And a falling training loss is not the same as a better model.

20
00:01:13,612 --> 00:01:15,612
You still need your held-out eval.

21
00:01:15,612 --> 00:01:19,725
Then there's a technique called LoRA, which is the breeder's restraint.

22
00:01:19,725 --> 00:01:25,050
Instead of changing the whole model, you freeze it, and you train a small add-on that sits beside it.

23
00:01:25,050 --> 00:01:30,937
The add-on is tiny, so it trains on a free graphics card and can be saved and swapped on its own.

24
00:01:30,937 --> 00:01:35,250
Several projects in the pack use this to build what they call decision models.

25
00:01:35,250 --> 00:01:42,062
Instead of writing an answer in words, the model scores the options you give it, and returns a choice with a probability.

26
00:01:42,062 --> 00:01:49,112
One small model went from about chance to around three quarters correct on its tests after a single short round of training.

27
00:01:49,112 --> 00:01:52,075
Another project warns that where you start matters.

28
00:01:52,075 --> 00:01:59,062
Training on a narrow set of examples from the bare base model wiped out much of what a ready-made decision model already knew.

29
00:01:59,062 --> 00:02:01,175
Here's where the metaphor breaks.

30
00:02:01,175 --> 00:02:04,662
Breeding selects among variation that already exists.

31
00:02:04,662 --> 00:02:06,687
Fine-tuning doesn't select.

32
00:02:06,687 --> 00:02:07,950
It copies.

33
00:02:07,950 --> 00:02:11,650
If your labels are wrong, the model learns them perfectly.

34
00:02:11,650 --> 00:02:15,350
In the lab, you'll do this for your own study-design task.

35
00:02:15,350 --> 00:02:25,225
Your coding agent will generate a few hundred practice examples, filter them with a second blind labeller, and build a free notebook that trains a small model in the cloud.

36
00:02:25,225 --> 00:02:32,200
Then you'll compare the untrained and trained models on your human-checked items from Module Twenty-Three, and nowhere else.

37
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:37,600
Start with Part A. Decide your rule for training data and test data, and write down why.

